    75	07-29/" data-passage="most participants supported maintaining the current target range" aria-label="Source 22: read the evidence" data-passages="Several participants favored an inc
    76	rease of 25 basis points">22</a></sup></p><p data-layer="narrative">By late August, Chair Kevin Warsh had set a public test: underlying inflation had to move toward the Fed's goal 
    77	“clearly and at sufficient speed.” The next official CPI release showed gasoline up 3.9 percent in August, accounting for more than one-third of the monthly increase, while core CP
    78	I was 2.4 percent over the year.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-23" data-record-href="/records/fed-warsh-jackson-hole-2026-08-28/" data-passage="clearly and at sufficient speed"
    79	 aria-label="Source 23: read the evidence">23</a></sup><sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-20" data-record-href="/records/bls-cpi-2026-09-11/" data-passage="gasoline rose 3.9 percen
    80	t in August, accounting for over one third" aria-label="Source 20: read the evidence" data-passages="all items less food and energy index rose 2.4 percent over the year">20</a></su
    81	p></p><p data-layer="narrative">Growth was not uniformly fast: real GDP increased at a 1.5 percent annual rate in the second quarter, down from 2.1 percent in the first. Private do
    82	mestic final sales, consumer spending plus private fixed investment, grew 4.2 percent in the same quarter.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-21" data-record-href="/records/bea-gdp-
    83	q2-second-estimate-2026-08-26/" data-passage="Real gross domestic product (GDP) increased at an annual rate of 1.5 percent" aria-label="Source 21: read the evidence" data-passages=
    84	"Real final sales to private domestic purchasers">21</a></sup></p><p data-layer="narrative">Warsh highlighted three considerations in explaining the September decision: the economy
    85	 had strengthened, his judgment that summer inflation trends had not passed the test remained, and the committee's geopolitical assessment had changed. The September statement desc
    86	ribed resilient domestic spending, robust capital investment and elevated inflation. Warsh described the increase as removing “a dose of accommodation” so financial and credit cond
    87	itions would be more consistent with the Fed's objectives.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-5" data-record-href="/records/fomc-press-conference-2026-09-16/" data-passage="I'll hig
