   106	rative">The mechanism</p><h2 data-layer="fact">What a rate hike can change</h2><p data-layer="narrative">The Fed raises the cost of borrowing to slow consumer and business spending
   107	, reducing demand.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-17" data-record-href="/records/pbs-ap-fed-consumer-effects-2026-09-16/" data-passage="slow consumer and business spending by ra
   108	ising the cost of borrowing" aria-label="Source 17: read the evidence">17</a></sup></p><p data-layer="narrative">The Associated Press traced the oil and gasoline shock to the war w
   109	ith Iran, which was in its seventh month by the day after the decision.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-24" data-record-href="/records/ap-reconstruction-2026-09-17/" data-passage
   110	="with the Iran war now in its seventh month" aria-label="Source 24: read the evidence">24</a></sup></p><div class="sourced-block" data-layer="fact" data-source="fomc-press-confere
   111	nce-2026-09-16"><p>A rate increase cannot set the price of oil or gasoline. Warsh said the Fed cannot affect an individual price; it can try to stop a relative-price shock from bro
   112	adening into second- and third-order effects across the economy.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-5" data-record-href="/records/fomc-press-conference-2026-09-16/" data-passage="We
   113	 cannot affect any individual price" aria-label="Source 5: read the evidence" data-passages="Don't have second and third order effects in the economy">5</a></sup></p><span class="c
   114	hip chip-record">record</span></div><p data-layer="narrative">The August CPI increase was partly an energy-price event: gasoline supplied more than a third of the monthly rise. War
   115	sh framed the Fed's task as preventing that relative-price shock from producing second- and third-order effects across the economy.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-20" data-recor
   116	d-href="/records/bls-cpi-2026-09-11/" data-passage="gasoline rose 3.9 percent in August, accounting for over one third" aria-label="Source 20: read the evidence">20</a></sup><sup c
   117	lass="src-ref"><a href="#src-5" data-record-href="/records/fomc-press-conference-2026-09-16/" data-passage="Don't have second and third order effects in the economy" aria-label="So
   118	urce 5: read the evidence">5</a></sup></p><p data-layer="narrative">The effect is delayed. The Fed's strategy statement says monetary policy influences activity, employment and pri
   119	ces with a lag, which creates a risk that an energy shock fades before tighter credit has fully slowed the economy.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-8" data-record-href="/records/
   120	fomc-longer-run-goals-2025/" data-passage="Monetary policy actions tend to influence economic activity, employment, and prices with a lag" aria-label="Source 8: read the evidence">
   121	8</a></sup><sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-24" data-record-href="/records/ap-reconstruction-2026-09-17/" data-passage="By the time higher borrowing costs started to slow the eco
   122	nomy, the gas price shock could be over" aria-label="Source 24: read the evidence">24</a></sup></p></section><section id="who-feels-it"><p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative"
   123	>Borrowers and savers</p><h2 data-layer="fact">Who feels it</h2><div class="sourced-block" data-layer="fact" data-source="pbs-ap-fed-consumer-effects-2026-09-16"><p>Credit-card rat
   124	es can respond quickly because most cards carry variable rates tied to banks' prime rate. Auto loans and adjustable-rate mortgages can also become more expensive, while savings acc
   125	ounts and certificates of deposit may pay more.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-17" data-record-href="/records/pbs-ap-fed-consumer-effects-2026-09-16/" data-passage="Fed's policy
   126	 changes flow through to credit card rates rapidly" aria-label="Source 17: read the evidence" data-passages="interest rates on savings accounts and certificates of deposit are head
   127	ed higher">17</a></sup></p><span class="chip chip-checked">checked</span></div><p data-layer="narrative">Fixed mortgage rates are different: they tend to follow the 10-year Treasur
   128	y yield, not the Fed's rate directly. Homeowners with fixed loans do not have their existing rate reset by this decision.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-17" data-record-href="/r
   129	ecords/pbs-ap-fed-consumer-effects-2026-09-16/" data-passage="Mortgage rates don't necessarily follow what the Fed does" aria-label="Source 17: read the evidence" data-passages="ma
   130	ny homeowners locked in low mortgage rates">17</a></sup></p><p data-layer="narrative">Consumer-finance analyst Matt Schulz told the Associated Press that one quarter-point increase
   131	 would not have a large effect. He said the larger burden would come if several increases stack up over time.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-17" data-record-href="/records/pbs-a
   132	p-fed-consumer-effects-2026-09-16/" data-passage="single quarter-point rate increase isn't really going to have a huge impact" aria-label="Source 17: read the evidence" data-passag
   133	es="stack a few these on top of each other over time">17</a></sup></p></section><section id="projections"><p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">The signal beyond the vote</p
