   168	layer="fact">What happened next</h2><div class="sourced-block" data-layer="fact" data-source="ap-reconstruction-2026-09-17"><p>The next day, the 10-year Treasury yield slipped. The
   169	 Associated Press described that as a likely sign that investors' inflation worries had eased, while noting that mortgage rates and other long-term borrowing costs are not directly
   170	 controlled by the Fed.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-24" data-record-href="/records/ap-reconstruction-2026-09-17/" data-passage="10-year Treasury yield even slipped a bit" ari
   171	a-label="Source 24: read the evidence" data-passages="The Fed’s rate can influence longer-term costs such as mortgage rates, but the Fed doesn’t directly control them">24</a></sup>
   172	</p><span class="chip chip-checked">checked</span></div><p data-layer="narrative">By September 18, CNBC reported that market-implied odds of another increase in October had risen t
   173	o about 58 percent from 42 percent a week earlier. Goldman Sachs expected an October increase, Bank of America expected increases in October and December, and one strategist said t
