   444	September 16, 2026              Chairman Warsh’s Press Conference                    PRELIMINARY
   445	
   446	
   447	       MICHELLE SMITH. Zach Halaschak.
   448	
   449	       ZACHARY HALASCHAK. Thanks for doing this, Mr. Chairman. Zach Halaschak, the
   450	
   451	Washington Examiner. I'm just curious, a couple weeks ago the president sent out a message
   452	
   453	essentially threatening to cut off trade to certain countries, unless rates were lowered. Obviously
   454	
   455	a unanimous decision to do the opposite. What would you say to investors who sort of believe
   456	
   457	this is another test of the Fed's independence? And then, kind of, when was the last time you
   458	
   459	spoke with the president? Do you anticipate a post-decision meeting, or--
   460	
   461	       CHAIRMAN WARSH. You gave me a long menu from which to choose. They're all
   462	
   463	very tempting. I don't have anything for you on discussions with the president. And I'm not a
   464	
   465	Wall Street newsletter. Part of the independence of the Federal Reserve is we stay in our lane.
   466	
   467	Independence is a two-way street. We'll let people that do trade policy and fiscal policy stay in
   468	
   469	their lane too. That's how we can stand up here and call them the way we see them.
   470	
   471	       MICHELLE SMITH. Brian Cheung.
   472	
   473	       BRIAN CHEUNG. Hi there, Brian Cheung with NBC News. Just wondering if you could
   474	
   475	explain who is the least well-off, and what does a rate hike do when those people might be
   476	
   477	pinched by higher mortgage rates, higher gas, higher grocery prices, and now broad higher rates?
   478	
   479	       CHAIRMAN WARSH. Yeah. It's a fair question. In macroeconomics, we tend to look at
   480	
   481	aggregates around here, aggregate GDP, overall labor market trends, the state of inflation. A lot
   482	
   483	of people in Washington spend a lot of time on distributional consequences, and that's their job
   484	
   485	and their business. What I was referring to in the least well-off tend to be people that don't own
   486	
