     7	## Exiting
     8	
     9	1. What happened: On September 16, the FOMC voted 12 to 0 to raise its target range by a quarter point to 3.75–4 percent, effective September 17. (`fomc-statement-2026-09-16`; `fomc-implementation-2026-09-16`)
    10	2. Why it matters to someone who does not follow this subject: The move can quickly raise variable borrowing costs and improve some savings yields, though one quarter point is modest and a series would matter more. (`pbs-ap-fed-consumer-effects-2026-09-16`)
    11	3. The easiest wrong reading, the one the headline or the coverage invites: The 4.1 percent median does not mean the committee decided to hold rates there through 2027 or promised its next move; the projections are individual, conditional judgments. (`cbs-fed-decision-2026-09-16`; `fomc-sep-2026-09-16`)
    12	4. The one concept a stranger must hold to follow it, the mechanism: Higher rates cannot make oil cheaper; they restrain demand and credit so an energy-price shock is less likely to spread into other prices and expectations, and they work with a lag. (`fomc-press-conference-2026-09-16`; `fomc-longer-run-goals-2025`)
    13	5. What changed: In July the committee held at 3.50–3.75 percent with three members seeking a hike; by September it was unanimous that a stronger economy, inflation that had not improved enough and a changed geopolitical outlook justified less accommodation, while the median projected 2026 rate rose from 3.8 to 4.1 percent. (`fomc-statement-2026-07-29`; `fomc-press-conference-2026-09-16`; `fomc-sep-2026-09-16`)
    14	6. What is unresolved as of the newest dated record: The record does not settle how many more increases will follow, whether the energy shock will last, or how much households will ultimately feel; the chair gave no forward guidance, markets and analysts disagreed, and the minutes were not yet available on September 19. (`cnbc-warsh-followup-2026-09-18`; `fomc-calendar-2026-09-19`)
    15	7. The next five questions a curious stranger asks, in order: What did the Fed do, and why now?; What can a rate hike change when oil helped drive inflation?; What changes for borrowers and savers?; Do the vote and projections mean more hikes are set?; How much does political pressure shape what comes next?
