   324	
   325	### What happened
   326	
   327	- `fomc-statement-2026-09-16`: decision, vote, range, resilient activity and timelier return to 2 percent.
   328	- `fomc-implementation-2026-09-16`: effective date separates the decision from implementation.
   329	- `fomc-statement-2026-07-29`: prior hold, 9–3 vote and three dissents for a hike.
   330	- `fomc-press-conference-2026-09-16`: stronger economy, insufficient inflation improvement, changed geopolitics and removal of accommodation.
   331	- Gap to admit: `audit-gap-july-minutes-2026-07-29`, the prior preference to wait for more information.
   332	- Gap to admit: `audit-gap-warsh-jackson-hole-2026-08-28`, the standard for acting.
   333	- Gap to admit: `audit-gap-bea-gdp-2026-q2`, the growth-composition baseline.
   334	
   335	### What a rate hike can change
   336	
   337	- `fomc-press-conference-2026-09-16`: the Fed cannot change an individual price and acts against second- and third-order effects.
   338	- `fomc-longer-run-goals-2025`: the target range is the main tool and policy works with a lag.
   339	- `fomc-statement-2026-07-29`: energy supply shocks were part of the prior inflation account.
   340	- Gap to admit: `audit-gap-bls-cpi-2026-09-11`, the gasoline contribution and core-CPI baseline.
   341	
   342	### Who feels it
   343	
   344	- `pbs-ap-fed-consumer-effects-2026-09-16`: variable borrowing, savings, autos, mortgages and the difference between one move and a series.
   345	- `fomc-press-conference-2026-09-16`: persistent inflation's burden on people living from wages without financial assets.
   346	
   347	### Projections / the real signal
   348	
   349	- `fomc-sep-2026-09-16`: September and June rate medians, 18-participant denominator and 12/4/2 dot distribution.
