    58	
    59	      <div data-layer="proof" class="event-record-context">
    60	        <p class="sources-line">This story rests on the September policy statement, implementation note, projections and press-conference transcript; the July statement and minutes; the Federal Reserve's policy framework and Jackson Hole speech; August CPI and second-quarter GDP releases; fixed rate-series files; and coverage checked against those records. The press-conference transcript is marked preliminary on every page. The Associated Press reconstruction was retained by the capture registry as extracted text and markdown, but its run did not retain a raw-body receipt. Its two attributed observations are used with that limitation recorded in the manifest and working note.</p>
    61	      </div>
    62	
    63	      <section id="what-happened">
    64	        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">The decision</p>
    65	        <h2 data-layer="fact">What happened</h2>
    66	
    67	        <SourcedBlock source="fomc-statement-2026-09-16" kind="record">
    68	          <p>The Federal Open Market Committee voted {event.kpis[1].value} to raise its target range by one-quarter percentage point, to {decision.range.lower}–{decision.range.upper.toFixed(2)} percent.<Cite s="fomc-statement-2026-09-16" passage="approved the following statement for release by a 12 – 0 vote" /><Cite s="fomc-statement-2026-09-16" passage="raise the target range for the federal funds rate by 1/4 percentage point" /></p>
    69	        </SourcedBlock>
    70	
    71	        <p data-layer="narrative">The new range took effect September 17. The Fed also raised the rate paid on reserve balances to 3.90 percent and the primary-credit rate to 4 percent.<Cite s="fomc-implementation-2026-09-16" passage="effective September 17, 2026" /><Cite s="fomc-implementation-2026-09-16" passage="increase in the primary credit rate to 4.0 percent" /></p>
    72	
    73	        <figure class="story-figure" data-layer="fact">
    74	          <figcaption><strong>The first increase since 2023</strong><span>Upper limit of the target range</span></figcaption>
    75	          <StepChart
    76	            {...ratePath}
    77	            yRange={[3.5, 5.75]}
    78	            annotations={[
    79	              { date: "2024-01-01", value: 5.5, text: "5.50%", accent: false },
    80	              { date: "2026-09-19", value: 4, text: "4.00%", anchor: "end", accent: true },
    81	            ]}
    82	            caption="The upper bound fell in six steps from September 2024 through December 2025, then rose a quarter point effective September 17, 2026."
    83	            ariaLabel="Federal funds target range upper limit from January 2024 through September 19, 2026"
    84	          />
    85	          <p class="chart-vintage" data-layer="proof">Computed from {seriesReceipt.rangeCount.toLocaleString("en-US")} daily DFEDTARU observations saved September 19.</p>
    86	        </figure>
    87	
    88	        <p data-layer="narrative">Seven weeks earlier, the committee had held the range at 3.50–3.75 percent by a {decision.julyVote.forHold}–{decision.julyVote.forIncrease} vote. The July minutes say most participants wanted more information before acting, while several already favored a quarter-point increase.<Cite s="fomc-statement-2026-07-29" passage="approved the following statement for release by a 9 – 3 vote" /><Cite s="fomc-minutes-2026-07-29" passage="most participants supported maintaining the current target range" /><Cite s="fomc-minutes-2026-07-29" passage="Several participants favored an increase of 25 basis points" /></p>
    89	
    90	        <p data-layer="narrative">By late August, Chair Kevin Warsh had set a public test: underlying inflation had to move toward the Fed's goal “clearly and at sufficient speed.” The next official CPI release showed gasoline up {inflation.augustGasolineChange} percent in August, accounting for {inflation.augustGasolineShare} of the monthly increase, while core CPI was {inflation.augustCoreCpiAnnual} percent over the year.<Cite s="fed-warsh-jackson-hole-2026-08-28" passage="clearly and at sufficient speed" /><Cite s="bls-cpi-2026-09-11" passage="gasoline rose 3.9 percent in August, accounting for over one third" /><Cite s="bls-cpi-2026-09-11" passage="all items less food and energy index rose 2.4 percent over the year" /></p>
    91	
    92	        <p data-layer="narrative">Growth was not uniformly fast: real GDP increased at a {growth.secondQuarterGdp} percent annual rate in the second quarter, down from {growth.firstQuarterGdp} percent in the first. Private domestic final sales, consumer spending plus private fixed investment, grew {growth.secondQuarterPrivateDomesticSales} percent in the same quarter.<Cite s="bea-gdp-q2-second-estimate-2026-08-26" passage="Real gross domestic product (GDP) increased at an annual rate of 1.5 percent" /><Cite s="bea-gdp-q2-second-estimate-2026-08-26" passage="Real final sales to private domestic purchasers" /></p>
    93	
    94	        <p data-layer="narrative">Warsh highlighted three considerations in explaining the September decision: the economy had strengthened, his judgment that summer inflation trends had not passed the test remained, and the committee's geopolitical assessment had changed. The September statement described resilient domestic spending, robust capital investment and elevated inflation. Warsh described the increase as removing “a dose of accommodation” so financial and credit conditions would be more consistent with the Fed's objectives.<Cite s="fomc-press-conference-2026-09-16" passage="I'll highlight three things that have happened in that intermeeting period" /><Cite s="fomc-statement-2026-09-16" passage="domestic spending has been resilient" /><Cite s="fomc-press-conference-2026-09-16" passage="We removed a dose of accommodation" /></p>
    95	
    96	        <SourcedBlock source="fomc-statement-2026-09-16" kind="record" detail>
    97	          <DataTable columns={["Line", "July 29", "September 16"]} rows={statementRows} caption="Selected statement lines, read directly from the two saved releases" />
    98	        </SourcedBlock>
    99	      </section>
   100	
   101	      <section id="what-rates-can-change">
   102	        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">The mechanism</p>
   103	        <h2 data-layer="fact">What a rate hike can change</h2>
   104	
   105	        <p data-layer="narrative">The Fed raises the cost of borrowing to slow consumer and business spending, reducing demand.<Cite s="pbs-ap-fed-consumer-effects-2026-09-16" passage="slow consumer and business spending by raising the cost of borrowing" /></p>
   106	
   107	        <p data-layer="narrative">The Associated Press traced the oil and gasoline shock to the war with Iran, which was in its seventh month by the day after the decision.<Cite s="ap-reconstruction-2026-09-17" passage="with the Iran war now in its seventh month" /></p>
   108	
   109	        <SourcedBlock source="fomc-press-conference-2026-09-16" kind="record">
   110	          <p>A rate increase cannot set the price of oil or gasoline. Warsh said the Fed cannot affect an individual price; it can try to stop a relative-price shock from broadening into second- and third-order effects across the economy.<Cite s="fomc-press-conference-2026-09-16" passage="We cannot affect any individual price" /><Cite s="fomc-press-conference-2026-09-16" passage="Don't have second and third order effects in the economy" /></p>
   111	        </SourcedBlock>
   112	
   113	        <p data-layer="narrative">The August CPI increase was partly an energy-price event: gasoline supplied more than a third of the monthly rise. Warsh framed the Fed's task as preventing that relative-price shock from producing second- and third-order effects across the economy.<Cite s="bls-cpi-2026-09-11" passage="gasoline rose 3.9 percent in August, accounting for over one third" /><Cite s="fomc-press-conference-2026-09-16" passage="Don't have second and third order effects in the economy" /></p>
   114	
   115	        <p data-layer="narrative">The effect is delayed. The Fed's strategy statement says monetary policy influences activity, employment and prices with a lag, which creates a risk that an energy shock fades before tighter credit has fully slowed the economy.<Cite s="fomc-longer-run-goals-2025" passage="Monetary policy actions tend to influence economic activity, employment, and prices with a lag" /><Cite s="ap-reconstruction-2026-09-17" passage="By the time higher borrowing costs started to slow the economy, the gas price shock could be over" /></p>
   116	      </section>
   117	
   118	      <section id="who-feels-it">
   119	        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">Borrowers and savers</p>
   120	        <h2 data-layer="fact">Who feels it</h2>
   121	
   122	        <SourcedBlock source="pbs-ap-fed-consumer-effects-2026-09-16" kind="checked">
   123	          <p>Credit-card rates can respond quickly because most cards carry variable rates tied to banks' prime rate. Auto loans and adjustable-rate mortgages can also become more expensive, while savings accounts and certificates of deposit may pay more.<Cite s="pbs-ap-fed-consumer-effects-2026-09-16" passage="Fed's policy changes flow through to credit card rates rapidly" /><Cite s="pbs-ap-fed-consumer-effects-2026-09-16" passage="interest rates on savings accounts and certificates of deposit are headed higher" /></p>
   124	        </SourcedBlock>
   125	
   126	        <p data-layer="narrative">Fixed mortgage rates are different: they tend to follow the 10-year Treasury yield, not the Fed's rate directly. Homeowners with fixed loans do not have their existing rate reset by this decision.<Cite s="pbs-ap-fed-consumer-effects-2026-09-16" passage="Mortgage rates don't necessarily follow what the Fed does" /><Cite s="pbs-ap-fed-consumer-effects-2026-09-16" passage="many homeowners locked in low mortgage rates" /></p>
   127	
   128	        <p data-layer="narrative">Consumer-finance analyst Matt Schulz told the Associated Press that one quarter-point increase would not have a large effect. He said the larger burden would come if several increases stack up over time.<Cite s="pbs-ap-fed-consumer-effects-2026-09-16" passage="single quarter-point rate increase isn't really going to have a huge impact" /><Cite s="pbs-ap-fed-consumer-effects-2026-09-16" passage="stack a few these on top of each other over time" /></p>
   129	      </section>
   130	
   131	      <section id="projections">
   132	        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">The signal beyond the vote</p>
   133	        <h2 data-layer="fact">The projections</h2>
   134	
   135	        <p data-layer="narrative">The median projected federal funds rate for the end of 2026 rose from {projections.june2026Median} percent in June to {projections.september2026Median} percent in September. For 2027, the median rose from 3.6 percent in June to {projections.september2027Median} percent in September.<Cite s="fomc-sep-2026-09-16" passage="Federal funds rate         4.1    4.1" /><Cite s="fomc-sep-2026-09-16" passage="June projection          3.8    3.6" /></p>
   136	
   137	        <p data-layer="narrative">The distribution shows several paths around the median: {projections.oneMoreIncrease} of {projections.participantCount} officials projected a single hike, {projections.twoMoreIncreases} projected two hikes, and {projections.noMoreIncrease} predicted a pair of holds. Warsh did not submit a projection of his own.<Cite s="the-hill-fed-decision-2026-09-16" passage="Twelve of 18 FOMC officials projected a single rate hike" /><Cite s="the-hill-fed-decision-2026-09-16" passage="Four officials projected two hikes, while two predicted a pair of holds" /><Cite s="fomc-press-conference-2026-09-16" passage="I have not offered a projection of" /></p>
   138	
   139	        <p data-layer="narrative">The projection document defines the dots as participants' individual assessments of appropriate monetary policy. Warsh said his practice is not to give forward guidance or prejudge future actions.<Cite s="fomc-sep-2026-09-16" passage="projections of participants’ individual assessments of appropriate monetary policy" /><Cite s="fomc-press-conference-2026-09-16" passage="My business is to not give forward guidance" /></p>
