   150	            <li>
   151	              <h3>The 2027 median was 4.1 percent.</h3>
   152	              CBS wrote that policymakers expected to hold rates steady throughout 2027. The record says something narrower: the median of individual projections was 4.1 percent in both years, about half of participants projected a steady rate next year, and the chair declined to give forward guidance.<Cite s="cbs-fed-decision-2026-09-16" passage="policymakers expect to hold rates steady throughout 2027" /><Cite s="cbs-fed-decision-2026-09-16" passage="About half of FOMC members predicted that rates would hold steady next year" /><Cite s="fomc-sep-2026-09-16" passage="For each period, the median is the middle projection" /><Cite s="fomc-press-conference-2026-09-16" passage="ill-prepared to pre-judge those future actions" />
   153	            </li>
   154	            <li>
   155	              <h3>One vote, eighteen projections.</h3>
   156	              Twelve committee members voted on the rate increase. Eighteen participants submitted projections. The vote applied to the September action; the projection record contains several paths after it.<Cite s="fomc-statement-2026-09-16" passage="12 – 0 vote" /><Cite s="fomc-sep-2026-09-16" passage="Eighteen participants submitted information" />
   157	            </li>
