   175	        <h2 data-layer="fact">What happened next</h2>
   176	
   177	        <SourcedBlock source="ap-reconstruction-2026-09-17" kind="checked">
   178	          <p>The next day, the 10-year Treasury yield slipped. The Associated Press described that as a likely sign that investors' inflation worries had eased, while noting that mortgage rates and other long-term borrowing costs are not directly controlled by the Fed.<Cite s="ap-reconstruction-2026-09-17" passage="10-year Treasury yield even slipped a bit" /><Cite s="ap-reconstruction-2026-09-17" passage="The Fed’s rate can influence longer-term costs such as mortgage rates, but the Fed doesn’t directly control them" /></p>
   179	        </SourcedBlock>
   180	
   181	        <p data-layer="narrative">By September 18, CNBC reported that market-implied odds of another increase in October had risen to about 58 percent from 42 percent a week earlier. Goldman Sachs expected an October increase, Bank of America expected increases in October and December, and one strategist said the move was not the start of an aggressive new tightening cycle.<Cite s="cnbc-warsh-followup-2026-09-18" passage="near 58% Friday morning" /><Cite s="cnbc-warsh-followup-2026-09-18" passage="We remain unconvinced that this is the start of an aggressive new tightening cycle" /></p>
