   191	        <ul data-layer="fact">
   192	          <li>Whether another increase follows this one. The projections are individual assessments of appropriate monetary policy, and Warsh declined to prejudge the next decision.<Cite s="fomc-sep-2026-09-16" passage="individual assessments of appropriate monetary policy" /><Cite s="fomc-press-conference-2026-09-16" passage="ill-prepared to pre-judge those future actions" /></li>
   193	          <li>How long the energy shock lasts. Monetary policy works with a lag, and the Associated Press described the risk that the gas-price shock could be over before higher borrowing costs started to slow the economy.<Cite s="ap-reconstruction-2026-09-17" passage="the gas price shock could be over" /><Cite s="fomc-longer-run-goals-2025" passage="influence economic activity, employment, and prices with a lag" /></li>
   194	          <li>How much households ultimately pay. The Associated Press analysis says several increases would stack up over time, while fixed mortgage rates can move differently from the policy rate.<Cite s="pbs-ap-fed-consumer-effects-2026-09-16" passage="stack a few these on top of each other over time" /><Cite s="pbs-ap-fed-consumer-effects-2026-09-16" passage="Mortgage rates don't necessarily follow what the Fed does" /></li>
   195	          <li>What the September deliberations add. The official calendar says regular-meeting minutes are released three weeks after the decision; its September minutes slot was blank in the September 19 capture.<Cite s="fomc-calendar-2026-09-19" passage="The minutes of regularly scheduled meetings are released three weeks after the date of the policy decision." /></li>
