5 Major, 1 Minor. No Critical findings. The reader model exists and was applied.

1. [Major] The headline KPI omits the period of the gasoline figure.

Page bytes: [byte 7,745](</Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-fed/dist/events/fed-rate/2026-09-16-unanimous-quarter-point-hike/index.html>) says `3.9% August gasoline increase`; [byte 13,605](</Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-fed/dist/events/fed-rate/2026-09-16-unanimous-quarter-point-hike/index.html>) says `gasoline up 3.9 percent in August`.

Record: [BLS pin lines 8-22](</Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-fed/data/sources/fed-rate/bls-cpi-2026-09-11.txt:8>) says the gasoline index rose 3.9 percent in August and frames it as part of the monthly CPI change. The same record says energy rose 16.3 percent over 12 months, with gasoline up 27.4 percent.

Correct account: 3.9 percent is the seasonally adjusted month-over-month gasoline change, not an annual rate. The KPI should state that denominator because the unlabeled number is a central premise of the story.

2. [Major] A reader in the default Story mode cannot see the B-graded PCE outlook.

Page bytes: [byte 1,356](</Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-fed/dist/events/fed-rate/2026-09-16-unanimous-quarter-point-hike/index.html>) hides `.sourced-block.detail` outside Facts and Work; [byte 25,782](</Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-fed/dist/events/fed-rate/2026-09-16-unanimous-quarter-point-hike/index.html>) places the PCE table in that hidden detail block.

Record: [SEP pin line 48](</Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-fed/data/sources/fed-rate/fomc-sep-2026-09-16.txt:48>) gives PCE medians of 3.7, 2.3, 2.1, 2.0, and 2.0 percent. The reader model grades this B material for answer 5.

Correct account: the default account should say that the median PCE outlook rose to 3.7 percent for 2026 from 3.6 percent in June, then falls to 2.3 percent in 2027. Hiding it leaves the rate path detached from the inflation outlook it is meant to explain.

3. [Major] The “Who feels it” section omits the B-graded group the chair identified as most exposed.

Page bytes: [byte 22,023](</Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-fed/dist/events/fed-rate/2026-09-16-unanimous-quarter-point-hike/index.html>) begins the section with variable-rate borrowers and savers; the section never identifies households without financial assets.

Record: [press-conference pin lines 479-517](</Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-fed/data/sources/fed-rate/fomc-press-conference-2026-09-16.txt:479>) says the least well-off are people without financial assets, home equity, or 401(k) equity who live paycheck to paycheck, and ties stable prices to real take-home-pay gains.

Correct account: borrowers and savers are not the whole distributional account. The page should explain that the chair identified households living on wages without financial assets as especially exposed to persistent inflation.

4. [Major] The open-questions section omits the next primary releases after the September 19 cutoff.

Page bytes: [byte 34,247](</Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-fed/dist/events/fed-rate/2026-09-16-unanimous-quarter-point-hike/index.html>) lists future decisions, the energy shock, household costs, and minutes, but not the September 30 PCE or GDP releases.

Record: [BEA GDP pin lines 13-16](</Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-fed/data/sources/fed-rate/bea-gdp-q2-second-estimate-2026-08-26.txt:13>) schedules the GDP third estimate and annual-account updates for September 30. [BEA’s July PCE release](https://bea.gov/news/2026/personal-income-and-outlays-july-2026) says August Personal Income and Outlays, including PCE, is due that day. The reader model grades this omission B for answer 6.

Correct account: September 30 was the next scheduled test of both the inflation premise and the growth baseline. It should appear alongside the unavailable minutes.

5. [Major] The page omits the relevant CBO primary record, leaving the energy mechanism under-specified.

Page bytes: [byte 20,300](</Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-fed/dist/events/fed-rate/2026-09-16-unanimous-quarter-point-hike/index.html>) calls the August CPI move “partly an energy-price event”; [byte 21,026](</Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-fed/dist/events/fed-rate/2026-09-16-unanimous-quarter-point-hike/index.html>) says the effect is delayed.

Record: CBO’s September 15 [Iran-cost analysis](https://www.cbo.gov/publication/62756) says disrupted oil, refining, and shipping raised petroleum prices; it estimates the conflict added 2.3 percentage points to annualized Q2 PCE inflation and would leave core PCE 0.3 points above CBO’s February projection in Q1 2027 because pass-through lasts longer than the direct gasoline effect. The reader model identifies CBO as B-grade independent context.

Correct account: the direct energy effect can fade while core-price pass-through persists. That distinction is material to the page’s central question of why a rate hike might follow an energy shock.

6. [Minor] Two core claims use outlet authority where pinned primary records establish them.

Page bytes: [byte 24,379](</Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-fed/dist/events/fed-rate/2026-09-16-unanimous-quarter-point-hike/index.html>) credits The Hill for the 12/4/2 dot distribution; [byte 29,521](</Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-fed/dist/events/fed-rate/2026-09-16-unanimous-quarter-point-hike/index.html>) credits it for Warsh’s “two-way street” statement.

Record: the pinned [SEP](https://www.federalreserve.gov/monetarypolicy/fomcprojtabl20260916.htm) gives 12 projections at 4.125, four at 4.375, and two at 3.875; the [press-conference pin](</Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-fed/data/sources/fed-rate/fomc-press-conference-2026-09-16.txt:467>) contains the exact independence statement.

Correct account: cite the primary SEP and transcript for those factual claims, retaining coverage only for its interpretation.

VERDICT: NO-SHIP


